  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate caravans 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The African Climate Justice Caravan is a youth- and civil society-led movement mobilizing voices and actions for climate justice across Africa. From September to December 2025, ACJC will organize national caravans in five countries&#x2014;Benin, Zambia, Chad, Nigeria, and Mali&#x2014;connecting grassroots communities, women, youth, and policymakers to demand equitable climate solutions.the initiative will:
- Engage over 5,000 participants directly through caravans, dialogues, and advocacy events.
- Amplify African climate justice narratives via media campaigns and regional platforms, reaching millions continent-wide.
- Influence policy processes at national, regional, and global levels, including African Union forums and COP30.
-Strengthen women&#x2019;s and youth leadership in climate governance and promote just transitions in food and energy systems.

By bridging local realities with global decision-making spaces, ACJC aims to position Africa as a united, influential voice in shaping a fair and sustainable climate future.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">JVE is a non-governmental organisation, created in 2001, seated in Lom&#xe9;, Togo. The organisation has over 40 local branches, and through affiliates in a total of 21 countries in Africa, JVE is today known as the biggest youth-oriented environmental movement on thecontinent. Starting in a small village, working with access to information and education concerning the degrading local environment, the organisation has expanded its geographical and thematic areas of work, but still in the domain of sustainable development. Itsactivities are focused around access to energy, water and sanitation services; cultural biodiversity and eco-conscience; climate change; and natural resource management.</narrative>
